\begin_layout Subsection
type
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Can be one of the following:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
"static" – The component does not change its image.
 Typically,
 this type is used to allow the user to click on it without triggering any other specific behavior.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
"stateN" – This is the most common type.
 The designer is responsible for updating the image whenever new data is received from the simulation.
 The designer should have all the images related to this specific component.
 For example,
 a 3-position switch (up/center/down) is a stateN type,
 and the designer should have images for each position:
 up,
 center,
 and down.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
"knobInteger" – This type is used for knobs with N (2 or more) rotation positions.
 The designer should have an image of the knob,
 and based on data from the simulation,
 the knob will rotate by X degrees from its default position (negative X degrees indicates counterclockwise rotation,
 while positive X degrees indicate clockwise rotation).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
“analogRotation” – This type is very similar to “knobInteger” except that this type receives analog input and using linear interpolation,
 convert the data to rotation.
 The knob is then rotated to the desired position.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
“analogVerticalTranslation” – Received analog values from the simulation and responsible to move the image up and down according to linear conversion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
“analogHorizontalTranslation” – Similar to “analogVerticalTranslation” while in this case – the image moves from left to right according to linear conversion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
“number” – Display a number received from the simulation.
 The designer is responsible for providing a list of images corresponding to each number and the maximum length of the number.
\end_layout

